# Practical No. 3(B)

## Aim
To demonstrate controlled and uncontrolled form components in React using `useState` and `useRef`.

## Code

### `src/App.js`

```jsx
import React from "react";
import FormComparison from "./components/FormComparison";

function App() {
  return <FormComparison />;
}

export default App;
```

### `src/components/FormComparison.jsx`

```jsx
import React, { useRef, useState } from "react";

function FormComparison() {
  const [controlledName, setControlledName] = useState("");
  const uncontrolledRef = useRef(null);

  const showValues = () => {
    alert(
      "Controlled: " + controlledName +
      "\nUncontrolled: " + uncontrolledRef.current.value
    );
  };

  return (
    <div>
      <h2>Controlled Input</h2>
      <input
        type="text"
        placeholder="Enter controlled name"
        value={controlledName}
        onChange={(event) =>
          setControlledName(event.target.value)
        }
      />

      <h2>Uncontrolled Input</h2>
      <input
        type="text"
        placeholder="Enter uncontrolled name"
        ref={uncontrolledRef}
      />

      <br /><br />

      <button onClick={showValues}>
        Show Values
      </button>
    </div>
  );
}

export default FormComparison;
```

## Output

- Controlled Input: Enter controlled name
- Uncontrolled Input: Enter uncontrolled name
- Click **Show Values** to display both entered values in an alert box.

## Result
Successfully demonstrated controlled and uncontrolled form components in React using `useState` and `useRef`.
